            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

During the blower door test, we had identified various locations where excess air leakage &amp; heat loss occurs:



** The current co-vented chimney (and the 5" open combustion air pipe) will be eliminated when you upgrade to high efficiency, direct-vent furnace &amp; hot water appliances.



** The chimney on the wood fireplace does not have a damper that fully closes &amp; there is a 4": open combustion air supply. These 2 holes will be eliminated when you install a direct vent wood stove insert into the location where the current open fireplace exists.



** The bottom sweep of the front entrance door does not seal well at all



** There was significant air leakage coming out from under the raised floor of the converted garage.



Savings: I modeled a 20% reduction in air leakage, that is estimated to save $ 552 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 16.3 GJ. If you were to proceed with an eligible wall insulation upgrade through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, this 20% reduction in air leakage would qualify for a $ 750 rebate.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of both the original house &amp; the converted garage are 2x6@16" (R20). We discussed your interest to update the exterior siding of this house and what the estimated energy savings would be if you were to add more insulation to the exterior of these walls, in conjunction with the new Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program that was launched on April 1, 2019. There are 3 tiers of rebates associated with how much insulation is added ($3,000 for adding R10 - R14, $5,000 for adding R15 - R19, and $8,000 for adding R20+). For your house, I modeled adding another R15 to the exterior of the walls.



Savings:

By increasing 100% of the exterior walls by R15, you could potentially save $ 870 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 25.7 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 5,000 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP).



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</mainWalls>
                <ceilings />
                <exposedFloors />
                <foundation>

The floor of the converted garage has the original uninsulated concrete slab with a raised (and uninsulated) floor above. You mentioned this floor is always cold and we identified significant air leakage coming out from under that floor into the adjacent basement.



As discussed, you should consider removing the existing floor &amp; subfloor and try to insulate &amp; air seal that existing broken garage floor slab (perhaps using a few inches of spray foam on the top of the concrete &amp; around the interior perimeter of the lower walls). Adding some batt insulation into the raised floor joists would also help make that room above feel warmer. I modeled adding R28 of total insulation value to that floor area.



Savings:

By adding R28 to the floor of the converted garage, you could potentially save $ 199 per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by 5.9 GJ. There are currently no rebates currently available for adding insulation to foundation floors.</foundation>
                <windows>

You house has 14 windows and most of them are older original windows. In conjunction with your planned wall insulation upgrade, this would be a good time to upgrade all of the windows with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified window rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend a full frame replacement window that is a triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged window, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.25 BTU per hour.



Savings: 

By replacing the older windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 454 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 13.4 GJ, and qualify for a $ 250 rebate per window (12 windows maximum rebate = $3,000) through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program. 



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms for DHERP, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</windows>
                <doors />
                <VentilationSystem />
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have a 121 litre oil-fired hot water tank (from August of 1999), co-vented together with the furnace up a chimney. You may want to consider replacing this hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



Savings: By replacing your current hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92, could potentially save $ 258 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 5.0 GJ and qualify for up to $ 1,250 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with an approximately 30+ year old oil furnace with a default 83% efficiency. In conjunction with a Deep Home Energy Retrofit of your house (more insulation &amp; less air leakage) I have modeled an upgrade from this older furnace to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing gas furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat.



Savings: By replacing your current furnace with a 95% AFUE high-efficiency condensing gas furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower, you could potentially save $ 2,772 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 51.0 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 3,000 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP). If you choose not to complete a DHERP eligible wall insulation upgrade, you would still be eligible for up to $ 1,500   from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

 

To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms for DHERP, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables />
                <WaterConservation>

Your house has two toilets and the one on the 2nd floor is an older unit that uses a lot of water &amp; it should be replaced with a lower water consumption model. When replacing toilets, purchase a 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model. Look for a MAP flush rating capacity of a minimum 800 - 100 grams per flush !!



Savings:

By replacing an older toilet with a new 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model, you could potentially save ~$50 per person, per year in water and energy savings.  There are currently no rebates available for toilets from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.</WaterConservation>
                <AdditionalComments>

Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the DHERP, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



The upgrade recommendations outlined within this report are estimated to qualify for up to $14,750 in rebates (not including the window rebates) from this program. The following are the estimated rebates per component of the DHERP:



     - $5,000 for the wall insulation

     - $3,000 for the windows ($250 per window for up to 12 windows)

     - $3,000 for the heating system

     - $   750 for the air leakage reduction

     - $3,000 for the bonus rebate



NOTE: If you do not proceed with the main floor wall insulation upgrade, than the DHERP does not apply - and the only rebates from the list above that would still apply would be the heating system rebate however it would only be 50% of what is listed above (or $1,500). So ............. in a way, there is a potential to receive up to $13,250 if you do choose to add a minimum R15 of main floor wall insulation !!



In addition to the estimated DHERP rebates outlined above, you are also eligible for other rebates from Energy Efficiency Rebate Program (http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program), including the following items that may be of interest to you for this house:

     - ESTAR washing machine - $400

     - ESTAR refrigerator - $400

     - ESTAR on-demand hot water heater - $1,250

     - Programmable Thermostat - 25% up to $175

     - Ventless Dryer - $400

     - LED Lighting - see next section.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Fort Simpson, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 400 (per fiscal year)  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



At the time of my visit, there were no exhaust fans vented to the outside (range hood is an interior recirculation model, the bathroom fan is vented into the basement and the dryer is venting inside during the winter months), so there was no need to perform a depressurization test. When you update the furnace, hot water &amp; wood fireplace to new "direct vent" equipment, there will no longer be any appliances in the house that are capable of back-drafting combustion gases into the house.



During my assessment of your home, I did see some smoke detectors that were not yet installed and a newer carbon monoxide (CO) detector. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has no active ventilation:

        - The kitchen range hood does not vent to the outside

        - There is one bathroom exhaust fan, however it vents into the basement

        - The clothes dryer is set up to vent inside the house during the winter

        - There was a 5" fresh air supply connected to the return air plenum of the furnace

                 *** However it has been disconnected &amp; plugged



NOTE: Your house is currently depending upon the 2 x 6" co-vented chimneys &amp; the 5" open combustion air pipe to help supplement the ventilation of this house. There is another 6" chimney on the wood fireplace (with a broken damper) and a 4" combustion air supply that both allow alot of heat loss. As a result of all of these open holes, your house is significantly over-ventilated.



You should have the 5" fresh air supply pipe reconnected directly to the return air plenum of the furnace with an integrated manual damper so you can adjust the amount of fresh air entering the house. Open it up further if the house is humid with condensation on the windows, and close it down further if the house is dry with lingering odours.



You should consider replacing the bathroom exhaust fan with an ENERGY STAR rated fan that use little electricity &amp; operate at low noise levels - and they have good internal dampers to reduce heat loss when they are not in use. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Fort Simpson



The following Nov. 1, 2019 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Fort Simpson:

Electricity - $18.90 min, $0.32 per kWh up to monthly subsidy threshold, $0.73 per kWh thereafter

Oil  - $1.27 per litre 

Propane - $0.69 per litre

Wood - $525.00 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $6.30 per bag



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your Energy Advisor



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Ken Baigent, Senior Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: Ken.Baigent@aea.nt.ca



